= false
> Spreading creates a new collection and leaves the original unchanged.
? mcq: What kind of copy does spreading create?
+ A shallow copy: element references are shared
- A deep copy of every nested object
- No copy, the same list is reused
- A frozen const copy
> Only the top-level collection is new; objects inside are shared.

@lesson dart-m05-l07 | Collection if and for | 10
## Learning objectives
- Build collections conditionally with collection `if`
- Generate elements with collection `for`
- Combine them for declarative data building

## Collection if
Inside a list, set or map literal, `if` includes an element only when a condition holds:

```dart
void main() {
  const isLoggedIn = true;
  const isAdmin = false;
  final menu = [
    'Home',
    if (isLoggedIn) 'Profile',
    if (isAdmin) 'Admin panel' else 'Help',
  ];
  print(menu); // [Home, Profile, Help]
}
```

## Collection for
`for` inside a literal generates elements:

```dart
void main() {
  final squares = [for (var i = 1; i <= 5; i++) i * i];
  final names = ['ada', 'grace'];
  final labels = [for (final n in names) 'Hello ${n.toUpperCase()}'];
  final lengths = {for (final n in names) n: n.length};
  print(squares); // [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]
  print(labels);  // [Hello ADA, Hello GRACE]
  print(lengths); // {ada: 3, grace: 5}
}
```

## Combining if, for and spread
```dart
void main() {
  final scores = {'Ada': 92, 'Bob': 55, 'Cy': 78};
  final honourRoll = [
    'Honour roll:',
    for (final MapEntry(:key, :value) in scores.entries)
      if (value >= 75) '$key ($value)',
  ];
  print(honourRoll); // [Honour roll:, Ada (92), Cy (78)]
}
```

## if-case in collections
Collection `if` also supports patterns:

```dart
Object? nickname = 'Ace';
final greeting = [
  'Welcome',
  if (nickname case String n when n.isNotEmpty) 'aka $n',
];
```

## Why it matters for Flutter
Flutter widget trees are built from lists of children. Collection `if` and `for` let you describe the UI declaratively:

```dart
// Column(children: [
//   const Header(),
//   if (isLoading) const CircularProgressIndicator(),
//   for (final item in items) ItemTile(item),
// ])
```

> TIP: Prefer collection `if`/`for` over building a list with `add` calls in a loop; the result reads like a description of the data.

:::check What does `[for (final x in [1, 2]) if (x > 1) x]` produce?
[2].
:::

## Common mistakes
- Putting an `if` *statement* with braces inside a literal; collection `if` takes a single element (or a spread).
- Trying to use `else if` chains that become unreadable; extract a variable instead.

## Summary
- Collection `if` conditionally includes elements (with optional `else`).
- Collection `for` generates elements in lists, sets and maps.
- Combine with spreads and patterns to build data declaratively.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Collections ▸ Control-flow operators
