@lesson dart-m05-l08 | Filtering | 8
## Learning objectives
- Filter collections with `where`, `whereType`, `takeWhile` and `skipWhile`
- Remove elements in place with `removeWhere` and `retainWhere`
- Choose between creating a new collection and mutating

## where
`where` returns a lazy iterable of elements that satisfy a test:

```dart
void main() {
  final products = [
    (name: 'Mouse', price: 25, inStock: true),
    (name: 'Monitor', price: 220, inStock: false),
    (name: 'Keyboard', price: 70, inStock: true),
  ];
  final affordable = products.where((p) => p.inStock && p.price < 100).map((p) => p.name);
  print(affordable.toList()); // [Mouse, Keyboard]
}
```

## whereType
`whereType<T>()` keeps elements of a type and gives you a properly typed iterable, great for mixed or nullable lists:

```dart
void main() {
  final mixed = <Object?>[1, 'two', 3, null, 'five'];
  final strings = mixed.whereType<String>(); // Iterable<String>
  final nonNull = [1, null, 3].whereType<int>(); // removes nulls
  print('${strings.toList()} ${nonNull.toList()}'); // [two, five] [1, 3]
}
```

Since Dart 3, `nonNulls` does the same for nullable iterables: `[1, null, 3].nonNulls`.

## takeWhile and skipWhile
These stop or start at the first element that fails the test:

```dart
final temps = [12, 15, 19, 24, 18, 26];
print(temps.takeWhile((t) => t < 20).toList()); // [12, 15, 19]
print(temps.skipWhile((t) => t < 20).toList()); // [24, 18, 26]
```

Note `18` appears after the first value ≥ 20, so `skipWhile` keeps it.

## Mutating filters
`removeWhere` and `retainWhere` modify a list in place:

```dart
final inbox = ['spam: win', 'hi mum', 'spam: free'];
inbox.removeWhere((m) => m.startsWith('spam'));
print(inbox); // [hi mum]
```

> TIP: In Flutter state, prefer creating a new filtered list (`where(...).toList()`) over mutating, so change detection can see a new object.

:::check What is the type of `[1, 'a'].whereType<int>()`?
Iterable<int>.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Forgetting `toList()` when you need a List (for example, to assign to a `List<String>` variable).
- Using `takeWhile` when you meant `where`: it stops at the first failure.

## Summary
- `where` filters lazily; `whereType<T>()` filters and casts.
- `takeWhile`/`skipWhile` work on leading runs of elements.
- `removeWhere`/`retainWhere` mutate in place.

## Further learning
- api.dart.dev ▸ Iterable.where
